uotes": [{"text": "He was not clever, poor fellow, he did not know what questions to ask; he asked the same questions again and again. He continued to show his own troubled thoughts, and the vague dread in his mind, […]", "reference": "1892, Margaret Oliphant, The Heir Presumptive and the Heir Apparent, page 263:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(of objects or actions) Showing mental quickness and resourcefulness.", "tags": [], "examples": ["This is a simple but clever trick to solve the problem."], "quotes": [{"text": "Private-equity nabobs bristle at being dubbed mere financiers.[…]Much of their pleading is public-relations bluster. Clever financial ploys are what have made billionaires of the industry’s veterans. “Operational improvement” in a portfolio company has often meant little more than promising colossal bonuses to sitting chief executives if they meet ambitious growth targets. That model is still prevalent today.", "reference": "2013 June 22, “Engineers of a different kind”, in The Economist, volume 407, number 8841, page 70:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(of objects or actions) Showing inventiveness or originality; witty.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Mr. Woodhouse was almost as much interested in the business as the girls, and tried very often to recollect something worth their putting in. \"So many clever riddles as there used to be when he was young--he wondered he could not remember them! but he hoped he should in time.\" And it always ended in \"Kitty, a fair but frozen maid.\"", "reference": "1816, Jane Austen, chapter 9, in Emma, volume 1:"}, {"text": "I felt they expected me to say clever things, and I never could think of any till after the party was over.", "reference": "1919, William Somerset Maugham, chapter III, in The Moon and Sixpence:"}, {"text": "Just before the break Villa were denied a second goal when Bent had the ball in the net, although he was ruled offside after Jean Makoun's clever pass.", "reference": "2011 April 10, Alistair Magowan, “Aston Villa 1-0 Newcastle”, in BBC Sport:"}, {"text": "The Rosenbloom Loop is a clever little device, but it’s an even more clever symbol of the role that discipline plays in the creation of illusion: the persistence of vision that makes sequential still images appear to move.", "reference": "2014 April 11, Ron Charles, “David Grand’s ‘Mount Terminus’”, in The Guardian Weekly, volume 190, number 18, page 37:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(UK, colloquial, chiefly in the negative) Fit and healthy; free from fatigue or illness.", "tags": ["UK", "colloquial"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "But at that moment I knew it was all over for me, I had never thought that this day would come, but it had and I was not feeling too clever. In fact I had to escape to a nearby toilet to be sick.", "reference": "2010, Colin Ross, Death of the Docks, page 196:"}, {"text": "Right, and no, because the solicitor has told her that Walter's legs didn't look too clever, which, apparently, everyone's putting down to a fall, thankfully.", "reference": "2019, Neil Forsyth, 30:07 from the start, in Guilt, season 1, episode 1, spoken by Jake McCall (Jamie Sives):"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(US, dated) Good-natured; obliging.", "tags": ["US", "dated"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(anthropology, of an Aboriginal Australian) Possessing magical abilities.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "When a clever man is out hunting and comes across the tracks of, say, a kangaroo, he follows them along and talks to the footprints all the time for the purpose of injecting magic into the animal which made them.", "reference": "1904, Journal & Proceedings of the Royal Society of New South Wales, volume XXXVIII, page 255:"}, {"text": "Prior to this, the two women, who were “clever,” and possessed a certain amount of magical “power,”[…].", "reference": "1947, Oceania,, volumes 16-17, page 330:"}, {"text": "Fred is the clever fellow or tribal doctor who practises with the Kuku-Yalanji people. The tribal doctor’s work includes curing sickness, finding out the causes of death, predicting the future and making and stopping rain.", "reference": "1991, John & Sue Erbacher, Aborigines of the Rainforest:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "schamanisch"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "zauberkundig"}, {"language": {"code": "sw", "name": "Swahili"}, "word": "erevu"}, {"language": {"code": "te", "name": "Telugu"}, "word": "మంత్రజాలం"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Fit; suitable; having propriety.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": ["18th c, Jonathan Swift, Alexander Pope (later lines), Imitation of Horace, 1852, Charles Knight (collator), Half-hours with the Best Authors, Volume 4, page 188,\nI can't but think 'twould sound more clever,\nTo me and to my heirs forever."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Well-shaped; handsome.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Tho' the Girl vvas a tight, clever VVench as any vvas, and thro' her pale Looks, you might diſcern Spirit and Vivacity, vvhich made her not indeed a perfect Beauty, but ſomething that vvas agreeable.", "reference": "1712, Humphry Polesworth [pseudonym; John Arbuthnot], “The Character of John Bull’s Sister Peg, with the Quarrels that Happen’d between Master and Miss, in Their Childhood”, in John Bull Still in His Senses: Being the Third Part of Law is a Bottomless-Pit. […], London: […] John Morphew, […], →OCLC, page 10:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["quick-witted", "sharp-witted", "cunning", "street-smart", "adroit", "talented", "ingenious", "crafty", "cunning", "magical"], "antonyms": ["dull", "stupid", "ineffectual", "naive", "clumsy", "antonym(s) of"]}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clever", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}